From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lycaos <caraboune@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 20:37:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] session-writeback-hard-off: /config owns server-side session
 storage

Writeback pushes whole sessions -- prompts, replies, tool calls and the
absolute working directory -- to https://code.grok.com, and can be armed
from three places the user never necessarily sees: --storage-mode
writeback, GROK_STORAGE_MODE, and writeback_enabled in the settings the
vendor backend serves. The only brake upstream ships is is_zdr_team(),
which this fork does not patch: is_data_collection_disabled() is the
gated predicate, and upstream deliberately routes user-facing sync
features around it (see its doc comment).

StorageMode::resolve now hands every privacy build to resolve_privacy,
where the single [cli].session_writeback switch decides and nothing else
does. init_remote_sync refuses to build a RemoteSync without it, so a
caller reaching Writeback another way still cannot upload. Asking for
more privacy is never refused: an explicit `local` beats the opt-in.

The switch is exposed in /config under Privacy ("Session sync to xAI
servers"), off by default, wired like the existing auto_update row. Its
description states what actually leaves the machine, and that inference
reaches xAI either way -- turning this off does not make the client
offline.

Gork-Patch-Id: session-writeback-hard-off
Gork-Invariant: session-writeback-opt-in-only
Gork-Risk: critical
